{
  "summary": "This scan found a worrying combination of changes made in the last 7 days. Defender's real-time protection was switched off, a new account (<account-3>) was created and given full administrator control, and a new automatic task called CacheCleaner now runs a program from a temporary folder. Each could have an innocent explanation, but together they match a common break-in pattern. First, confirm whether you or someone you trust made these changes. If not, treat the computer as possibly compromised and follow the fixes shown in the report, starting with Defender.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Defender real-time protection was turned off since the last scan",
      "finding_ids": [
        "DEF-REALTIME-OFF:6ed445bc",
        "SETTING-WEAKENED:916db687"
      ],
      "explanation": "Microsoft Defender's real-time protection is off now, and the previous scan 7 days ago shows it was on. Defender itself is still enabled, its virus definitions are up to date, and tamper protection is on. However, with real-time protection off, harmful files are not blocked when they arrive. Turning this off is often one of the first things malware or an intruder does. If you didn't turn it off yourself, take that seriously. The fix shown in the report explains how to turn it back on."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "New account <account-3> created and made an administrator",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-ADMIN:29255107",
        "NEW-USER:8187239c",
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:feb6df5d"
      ],
      "explanation": "These three findings are all about the same account, <computer>\\<account-3>. It did not exist at the last scan. It is now enabled and has administrator rights, which means it can change anything on the computer. If you or someone in your household created it on purpose, this is fine. If nobody recognizes it, it may be a way back in for whoever created it, especially since it appeared during the same week Defender's protection was switched off. See the fixes in the report for these findings."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New automatic task 'CacheCleaner' running from a temporary folder",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:658daf42"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task named CacheCleaner was added since the last scan. It is set to run a program called cc.exe from your user Temp folder (C:\\Users\\<user>\\AppData\\Local\\Temp). It runs under your account with limited (non-administrator) rights. Some normal apps do start from folders under your user profile. But a Temp folder is an unusual place for a program to run from every time, and the task appeared alongside the other changes above. If you don't recognize it, the report shows how to check and remove it."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "Existing administrator account <account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> has administrator rights. It was not flagged as new, so it was already present at the last scan. Having a separate administrator account is common and often good practice. Just confirm it is one you know about. This is a routine check, not a sign of a problem by itself."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "The task runs a program from a Temp folder, not a normal app install location. It also appeared in the same 7-day window as Defender's real-time protection being turned off and a new administrator being added. Taken together, it deserves the same urgency as those high findings."
    }
  ]
}
